\chapter{Metrics}
\label{metrics}

The log records what a student did; the metrics in this chapter say
something about \emph{how} they did it.
Three families are computed, all from data the log already holds:
\begin{description}
\item[Time and effort] how many days the student worked, how many runs
  and sessions those days held, how much code changed, and how long the
  run-to-run activity lasted (\cref{sec:metrics-time}).
\item[Error behaviour] how often a run that ended in an error was
  followed by another error, and whether it was the same error: the
  error quotient and repeated error density
  (\cref{sec:metrics-eq,sec:metrics-red}).
\item[Code] the size and branching of the program as it stands, or as
  it stood at the end of each week, plus whatever a linter finds
  (\cref{sec:metrics-code,sec:metrics-lint}).
\end{description}
A fourth command, [[trends]], joins the three into one table over
time and says, per metric, whether it is rising, falling or steady
(\cref{sec:metrics-trends}).

Every one of these numbers is for the student to look at their own
history with, and nothing else.
The literature is clear on what they are not: compilation-behaviour
metrics failed to identify at-risk students even in the hands of their
inventors~\autocite{tabanao2011predicting}; their values shift with the
environment that produced them, so they cannot be compared with
published cohorts~\autocite{karvelas2020effects}; and coarse
time-on-task barely correlates with course
results~\autocite{leinonen2022timeontask,edwards2009comparing}.
What survives that evidence is \emph{self-referenced description}: this
week beside the student's own earlier weeks, in numbers with no
judgement attached---which is also the reference frame learners
receive best~\autocite{jivet2018license}.
The wording rules in \cref{sec:metrics-trends} and the header lines
every report prints exist to keep the output inside that frame.
The literature behind each claim, with the searches that found it and
the searches that looked for its refutation, is in the appendix; that
appendix is hand-written \LaTeX{} rather than a literate source,
because it documents a search, not a program.

\ltnote{The chapter follows the data flow rather than the command
list: records, then the two derived structures every metric shares
(error events and sessions), then the metrics, then the periods that
slice them, and only at the end the rendering.  A reader who wants one
metric can enter at its section; a reader who wants to change what a
\enquote{session} is finds it once, before any metric depends on it.}


\section{Code overview}

The module is pure computation: it reads a repository through the
functions of \cref{commits} and \cref{progsnap2}, and returns plain
data structures that the command-line layer (\cref{cli}) renders.
Nothing here imports Typer, and nothing here writes to the log.

