Most tests build records by hand, because every metric is a function of
the record fields alone and a Git repository is only needed to test the
reading step.
The factory fills in everything a test does not care about:

<<metrics test helpers>>=
T0 = datetime.datetime(2026, 8, 31, 10, 0)

def _rec(minutes, *, end=1, script="main.py", tree=None,
         exc=None, exit_code=0, added=0, removed=0):
  """A run starting `minutes` after `T0` lasting `end` minutes."""
  start = T0 + datetime.timedelta(minutes=minutes)
  finish = None if end is None else \
    start + datetime.timedelta(minutes=end)
  return RunRecord(
    commit=f"c{minutes}", tree=tree or f"t{minutes}",
    script=script, arguments="", start=start, end=finish,
    exit_code=exit_code, exception_info=exc,
    lines_added=added, lines_removed=removed,
  )

SYNTAX_TRACE = (
  '  File "main.py", line 3\n'
  "    print(x\n"
  "         ^\n"
  "SyntaxError: '(' was never closed\n"
)
NAME_TRACE = (
  "Traceback (most recent call last):\n"
  '  File "/usr/lib/python3.13/runpy.py", line 88, in _run_code\n'
  "    exec(code, run_globals)\n"
  '  File "main.py", line 2, in <module>\n'
  "    print(x)\n"
  "NameError: name 'x' is not defined\n"
)

def _syntax(minutes, **kw):
  return _rec(minutes, exc=SYNTAX_TRACE, exit_code=1, **kw)

def _name(minutes, **kw):
  return _rec(minutes, exc=NAME_TRACE, exit_code=1, **kw)
@


\section{From the log to run records}
\label{sec:metrics-records}

A run lives in two commits (\cref{sec:run-join}); the metrics want it
as one value with the fields they read.
[[RunRecord]] is that value.
It carries the tree hash of the header commit, because the error
quotient needs to know whether the code changed between two runs and
comparing tree object ids answers that exactly and for free
(\cref{sec:metrics-eq}); and it carries the line counts of the edit
that preceded the run, because lines changed is one of the effort
counts (\cref{sec:metrics-time}).

<<metrics imports>>=
import collections
import dataclasses
import datetime
import enum
import re
<<metrics functions>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class RunRecord:
    """One recorded run of the student's program.

    `start` and `end` are naive local datetimes; `end`,
    `exit_code` and `exception_info` are `None` when the run
    never finalised.  `lines_added` and `lines_removed` count
    the edit between this run's code state and the previous
    commit's.
    """

    commit: str
    tree: str
    script: str | None
    arguments: str | None
    start: datetime.datetime
    end: datetime.datetime | None
    exit_code: int | None
    exception_info: str | None
    lines_added: int = 0
    lines_removed: int = 0

    @property
    def command(self):
        """The command line as the log recorded it."""
        return f"{self.script or ''} {self.arguments or ''}".strip()
@

Two facts about a commit are not in its message: its tree, and the
size of the change it made.
Both come from one [[git log]] call each over the whole recording
branch, which is the same shape as [[scan_run_commits]]
(\cref{sec:run-join}) and for the same reason---a listing must not
cost one fork per commit.
The numstat output names the commit on a line of its own and then one
[[added\trmoved\tpath]] line per file, with [[-]] for binary files,
which we count as zero.

<<metrics functions>>=
def commit_trees(repo):
    """Map every commit on the recording branch to its tree hash."""
    result = repo.git(
        "log", "--format=%H %T", repo.recording_branch(),
        check=False,
    )
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return {}
    trees = {}
    for line in result.stdout.split("\n"):
        parts = line.split()
        if len(parts) == 2:
            trees[parts[0]] = parts[1]
    return trees
<<metrics functions>>=
def commit_churn(repo):
    """Map every commit to the `(added, removed)` lines of its diff."""
    result = repo.git(
        "log", "--numstat", "--format=%H", repo.recording_branch(),
        check=False,
    )
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return {}
    churn = {}
    current = None
    for line in result.stdout.split("\n"):
        if NUMSTAT_LINE.match(line):
            added, removed, _ = line.split("\t", 2)
            old = churn.get(current, (0, 0))
            churn[current] = (
                old[0] + (int(added) if added.isdigit() else 0),
                old[1] + (int(removed) if removed.isdigit() else 0),
            )
        elif COMMIT_HASH_LINE.match(line):
            current = line.strip()
    return churn
<<metrics constants>>=
NUMSTAT_LINE = re.compile(r"^(\d+|-)\t(\d+|-)\t")
COMMIT_HASH_LINE = re.compile(r"^[0-9a-f]{40}$")
@

The recorder writes [[datetime.now().isoformat()]], a naive local time,
and every comparison in this chapter is between such values.
A history that went through a ProgSnap2 round trip or another machine
may carry an offset, so timestamps are normalised on the way in rather
than compared as they come: an aware value is moved to local time and
its offset dropped, a naive one is trusted.
[[utcnow]] never appears, because the student's day boundaries are
local ones.

<<metrics functions>>=
def local_naive(value):
    """Return `value` as a naive local datetime, or `None`."""
    if value is None:
        return None
    if value.tzinfo is not None:
        return value.astimezone().replace(tzinfo=None)
    return value
@

Reading the log combines the three per-branch scans with the
per-commit parsing the exporter already does.
Learnlog's own runs are dropped by default: a [[learnlog list]] is not
work on the program, and the [[learnlog metrics]] invocation that is
running right now already has its header commit in the log---counting
it would make every report inflate the next.
This is [[is_learnlog_run]], not [[filter_internal_runs]]
(\cref{sec:filter-learnlog}), because the latter deliberately keeps the
review runs that listing and playback want to show.
A commit's diff is the edit made before its first kept run, so the
churn is attributed to that run and to no other run of the same commit.

