\section{What counts as an error}
\label{sec:metrics-errors}

The error metrics were defined over compiler events: in BlueJ a
compilation is the student asking the toolchain to judge their code,
and the metric scores what the judgement said
\autocite{jadud2006methods}.
For a Python script the same speech act is [[python main.py]], and
its verdict is whatever traceback the run left behind.
That is the only evidence used.
The exit status is not: [[sys.exit(1)]] ends a run non-zero with no
error in sight, and a caught exception ends it with zero after an
error the student may well have been fighting.

Python blurs the compile/run line the metrics were drawn on, but not
beyond recovery.
The interpreter compiles the whole file before executing any of it, so
a [[SyntaxError]] (or its subclasses [[IndentationError]] and
[[TabError]]) is the compile-time verdict and everything else is a
run-time one.
Both matter: scored separately, syntax errors carried signal early in
a course and runtime errors later~\autocite{svabensky2024comparison},
which is why every error metric below is computed twice, over
syntax errors alone and over all uncaught exceptions.
An interruption---[[KeyboardInterrupt]], or a [[SystemExit]] that
escaped---is the student stopping the program, not the program
failing, and belongs to neither series.

The classification keys on the exception \emph{type}, not on the
traceback text.
A syntax error in the script itself arrives without the
[[Traceback (most recent call last):]] header, because no frame ever
ran; one raised by [[exec]] inside a running program arrives with one.
The header is recorded as a field for anyone who wants it, but a rule
built on it would misfile the second case.

<<metrics constants>>=
SYNTAX_EXCEPTIONS = frozenset({
    "SyntaxError", "IndentationError", "TabError",
})
INTERRUPT_EXCEPTIONS = frozenset({"KeyboardInterrupt", "SystemExit"})
TRACEBACK_HEADER = "Traceback (most recent call last):"
<<metrics functions>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class ErrorEvent:
    """How a run ended.

    `kind` is `"none"`, `"syntax"`, `"runtime"` or
    `"interrupt"`; `location` is `(file, line)` of the deepest
    frame in the student's own code, when there is one.
    """

    kind: str
    type: str | None
    message: str | None
    location: tuple[str, int] | None
    has_traceback_header: bool
<<metrics functions>>=
def classify_error(record):
    """Classify the traceback a run left, if any."""
    info = record.exception_info
    if not info:
        return ErrorEvent("none", None, None, None, False)
    exc_type, message = parse_exception_line(info)
    if exc_type in SYNTAX_EXCEPTIONS:
        kind = "syntax"
    elif exc_type in INTERRUPT_EXCEPTIONS:
        kind = "interrupt"
    else:
        kind = "runtime"
    return ErrorEvent(
        kind, exc_type, message, deepest_frame(info),
        TRACEBACK_HEADER in info,
    )
@

The location the error quotient compares is where the student's code
failed, not where the interpreter noticed.
A traceback lists frames outermost first, so the deepest frame is the
last [[File "...", line N]] line---but the last frame is often inside
the standard library or a package, which the student did not write and
will not edit.
We therefore take the last frame outside those places, and fall back
to the last frame of all when every frame is foreign.

<<metrics constants>>=
FRAME_LINE = re.compile(
    r'^\s*File "(?P<file>[^"]+)", line (?P<line>\d+)', re.M,
)
FOREIGN_FRAME_MARKERS = ("site-packages", "/lib/python", "<frozen")
<<metrics functions>>=
def deepest_frame(exception_info):
    """Return `(file, line)` of the deepest frame in the student's code."""
    frames = [
        (match.group("file"), int(match.group("line")))
        for match in FRAME_LINE.finditer(exception_info)
    ]
    own = [
        frame for frame in frames
        if not any(marker in frame[0]
                   for marker in FOREIGN_FRAME_MARKERS)
    ]
    if own:
        return own[-1]
    return frames[-1] if frames else None
@

The two series share one predicate, parameterised by which errors
count: [[kind="syntax"]] for the compile-time series, [[kind="any"]]
for both.

<<metrics functions>>=
def is_error(event, kind):
    """True if `event` counts as an error in the `kind` series."""
    if kind == "syntax":
        return event.kind == "syntax"
    return event.kind in ("syntax", "runtime")
@

Let's verify each shape of ending: a syntax error without header, a
runtime error whose deepest own frame is below a library frame, an
interruption, and a non-zero exit with no traceback at all.

<<metrics test functions>>=
def test_classify_error_syntax_without_header():
  event = classify_error(_syntax(0))
  assert event.kind == "syntax"
  assert event.type == "SyntaxError"
  assert event.location == ("main.py", 3)
  assert not event.has_traceback_header

def test_classify_error_runtime_uses_own_deepest_frame():
  event = classify_error(_name(0))
  assert event.kind == "runtime"
  assert event.type == "NameError"
  assert event.message == "name 'x' is not defined"
  assert event.location == ("main.py", 2)
  assert event.has_traceback_header

def test_classify_error_interrupt_and_plain_exit():
  interrupted = classify_error(
    _rec(0, exc="Traceback (most recent call last):\n"
                "  File \"main.py\", line 1, in <module>\n"
                "KeyboardInterrupt\n"),
  )
  assert interrupted.kind == "interrupt"
  assert interrupted.type == "KeyboardInterrupt"
  assert classify_error(_rec(0, exit_code=1)).kind == "none"
  assert not is_error(classify_error(_rec(0, exit_code=1)), "any")
  assert is_error(classify_error(_name(0)), "any")
  assert not is_error(classify_error(_name(0)), "syntax")
