\section{Sessions}
\label{sec:metrics-sessions}

Both the time metrics and the error metrics are defined over a
\emph{session}: a stretch of work with no long break in it.
Nothing in the log marks where one ends, so a threshold decides: a gap
of at least the idle threshold between the end of one run and the
start of the next opens a new session.

The threshold is twenty minutes.
That is the convention Price et al.\ apply to ProgSnap2 data without a
session column, following Petersen et al.\
\autocite{price2020progsnap2,petersen2015exploration}, and it is the
only one defined for events as sparse as program runs.
The values in the keystroke literature are far smaller---five minutes
in one validation against self-report~\autocite{hart2023accurate},
ten where the session count was seen to plateau
\autocite{leinonen2021finegrained}, three in our own earlier work on
keystroke-scale data~\autocite{snider2023edit}---and they are not
transferable: the threshold scales with how sparse the events are, and
at run granularity a three-minute cut would shatter every session.
Because the choice of estimator moves results
materially~\autocite{kovanovic2015penetrating}, the threshold is a
parameter and every report prints the value it used.

The gap is measured from the end of a run, not its start, so a program
that ran for eight minutes does not spend the idle budget while it
runs.
A run that never finalised has no end; its start stands in.

<<metrics constants>>=
DEFAULT_IDLE_MINUTES = 20
<<metrics functions>>=
def segment_sessions(records, idle):
    """Group chronological `records` into sessions.

    A gap of at least `idle` (a timedelta) between one run's end
    and the next run's start starts a new session.
    """
    sessions = []
    for record in records:
        if sessions:
            previous = sessions[-1][-1]
            gap = record.start - (previous.end or previous.start)
            if gap >= idle:
                sessions.append([record])
                continue
            sessions[-1].append(record)
        else:
            sessions.append([record])
    return sessions
@

Let's verify the boundary exactly: a gap one second short of the
threshold keeps the session, the threshold itself ends it, and the gap
is counted from the run's end.

<<metrics test helpers>>=
IDLE = datetime.timedelta(minutes=DEFAULT_IDLE_MINUTES)
<<metrics test functions>>=
def test_segment_sessions_splits_at_threshold():
  runs = [_rec(0), _rec(20), _rec(41), _rec(61, end=None), _rec(81)]
  sessions = segment_sessions(runs, IDLE)
  # 0 ends 10:01, 20 starts 10:20: gap 19 min -> same session.
  # 20 ends 10:21, 41 starts 10:41: gap 20 min -> new session.
  # 41 ends 10:42, 61 starts 11:01: 19 min -> same session.
  # 61 never ended: its start 11:01 -> 81 starts 11:21: 20 -> new.
  assert [[r.start.minute for r in s] for s in sessions] == \
    [[0, 20], [41, 1], [21]]
  assert segment_sessions([], IDLE) == []
@


\section{Time on task}
\label{sec:metrics-time}

Kovanović et al.\ name the two problems every log-based time estimate
faces: an \emph{outlier}---a gap that spans time off task---and the
\emph{last action}, whose duration no later event
reveals~\autocite{kovanovic2015penetrating}.
Sessions dispose of the first.
The two-commit protocol disposes of the second by construction: a
learnlog run has a trailer, so the duration of the last action in a
session is measured, not estimated.
This is a real advantage of recording a header before and a trailer
after each run (\cref{sec:run-join}), and it is why the run's own
duration is counted.

Counted with a cap: a program blocked on [[input()]] for forty minutes
is wall-clock, not engagement, and the idle threshold is the largest
duration we are prepared to believe.
Active time in a session is therefore the runs' capped durations plus
the gaps between them, each of which is below the threshold by
construction.
Nothing is credited before the first run---the coarse convention
\autocite{leinonen2022timeontask}---so the number is honestly labelled
\emph{active time, run-to-run}, never \emph{time spent}.

<<metrics constants>>=
ZERO = datetime.timedelta()
<<metrics functions>>=
def active_time(session, idle):
    """Run-to-run active time of one session, as a timedelta."""
    total = ZERO
    for index, run in enumerate(session):
        if run.end is not None:
            total += min(max(run.end - run.start, ZERO), idle)
        if index + 1 < len(session):
            gap = session[index + 1].start - (run.end or run.start)
            total += max(gap, ZERO)
    return total
@

Time is not the headline.
Within the same students, better-scored work was started earlier, not
worked on longer~\autocite{edwards2009comparing}; coarse time-on-task
did not correlate with weekly points at
all~\autocite{leinonen2022timeontask}; and log-derived time deviates
from screen-recorded ground truth~\autocite{krieter2022areyoustillthere}.
What does track active time are the counts---runs ($r=.53$) and edits
($r=.72$)~\autocite{leinonen2017comparison}---so the summary leads
with days, when the first run fell, runs, sessions, failed runs and
lines changed, and puts the clock last.
A run that never finalised is counted and reported as incomplete
rather than silently treated as instantaneous.

<<metrics functions>>=
def time_summary(sessions, idle):
    """Effort counts and active time over `sessions`, as a dict."""
    runs = [run for session in sessions for run in session]
    seconds = sum(
        (active_time(session, idle) for session in sessions), ZERO,
    ).total_seconds()
    return {
        "active_days": len({run.start.date() for run in runs}),
        "first_run": min((run.start for run in runs), default=None),
        "runs": len(runs),
        "sessions": len(sessions),
        "failed_runs": sum(
            is_error(classify_error(run), "any") for run in runs
        ),
        "lines_added": sum(run.lines_added for run in runs),
        "lines_removed": sum(run.lines_removed for run in runs),
        "active_seconds": seconds if runs else None,
        "incomplete_runs": sum(run.end is None for run in runs),
    }
@

Let's verify the cap, the gap, and that nothing is credited before the
first run: a 45-minute run counts 20, a 5-minute gap counts whole, and
two runs a minute long with that gap sum to seven minutes.

<<metrics test functions>>=
def test_active_time_caps_runs_and_credits_gaps():
