\section{Error quotient}
\label{sec:metrics-eq}

Jadud noticed, reading novices' compilation logs by hand, that the
students who struggled met the same error again and
again~\autocite{jadud2006methods}.
The error quotient turns that into a number: pair each event with the
next, score each pair for how much the second error repeats the first,
normalise by the largest score a pair can have, and average.
His scoring cascade has four terms---both events errors, same error
type, same line (within one), same edit location---worth $2+3+3+1$,
so the divisor is nine~\autocite{jadud2005thesis}.
(Secondary accounts often drop the fourth term and then divide by nine
anyway; the thesis figure has all four.)
The thesis also filters out pairs between which the code did not
change: a re-run of the same file is the same verdict twice, not a
repeated mistake.

The parameters are not universal.
Petersen et al.\ explored them across C, Java and Python courses and
found the algorithm sensitive to its
context~\autocite{petersen2015exploration}; Ahadi et al.\ list the
free parameters as the metric's standing
caveat~\autocite{ahadi2018syntax}.
For run-based Python data our own earlier work uses a two-parameter
model---a penalty of 13 when both runs error and 3 more when the
error type repeats, normalised by 16---attributed there to Petersen et
al.'s exploration~\autocite{snider2023edit}.
That is the default here, named in every report; the four-term
original is implemented beside it so the citation-faithful formula is
available, and so that the difference is visible in the code rather
than in a footnote.

<<metrics constants>>=
EQ_BOTH_ERROR_PENALTY = 13
EQ_SAME_TYPE_PENALTY = 3
EQ_MAX_PAIR_SCORE = 16
EQ_VARIANT = (
    "two-parameter EQ: both runs error +13, same error type +3, "
    "normalised by 16"
)
JADUD_TERMS = (2, 3, 3, 1)
JADUD_MAX_PAIR_SCORE = sum(JADUD_TERMS)
JADUD_LINE_TOLERANCE = 1
@

Which runs form a pair?
Watson's revision of the idea pairs events per file, because a student
who compiles a different file has not resolved the error in the first
one~\autocite{watson2015thesis}; learnlog records the script each run
executed, so pairs are consecutive runs of the same script within a
session.
Jadud's zero-edit filter is exact here: two header commits with the
same tree hash enclose no change at all.
It is stricter than Watson's tokenised comparison---a comment-only
edit survives ours and not his---which the prose of the report should
not hide.

<<metrics functions>>=
def run_pairs(session):
    """Consecutive runs of the same script with a change between."""
    chains = collections.defaultdict(list)
    for run in session:
        chains[run.script].append(run)
    pairs = []
    for chain in chains.values():
        for first, second in zip(chain, chain[1:]):
            if first.tree and first.tree == second.tree:
                continue
            pairs.append((first, second))
    return pairs
<<metrics functions>>=
def score_pair(first, second, kind):
    """Two-parameter EQ score of one pair, in $[0, 1]$."""
    before, after = classify_error(first), classify_error(second)
    if not (is_error(before, kind) and is_error(after, kind)):
        return 0.0
    score = EQ_BOTH_ERROR_PENALTY
    if before.type == after.type:
        score += EQ_SAME_TYPE_PENALTY
    return score / EQ_MAX_PAIR_SCORE
@

The fourth of Jadud's terms asks whether the student edited the same
place both times.
Runs do not record where the edit was, so the caller says whether it
knows; the default is that it does not, and the report prints as much.

<<metrics functions>>=
def score_pair_jadud(first, second, kind, *,
                     same_edit_location=False):
    """Jadud's original four-term EQ score of one pair."""
    before, after = classify_error(first), classify_error(second)
    if not (is_error(before, kind) and is_error(after, kind)):
        return 0.0
    both, same_type, same_line, same_edit = JADUD_TERMS
    score = both
    if before.type == after.type:
        score += same_type
    if (
        before.location and after.location
        and before.location[0] == after.location[0]
        and abs(before.location[1] - after.location[1])
        <= JADUD_LINE_TOLERANCE
    ):
        score += same_line
    if same_edit_location:
        score += same_edit
    return score / JADUD_MAX_PAIR_SCORE
@

Jadud averaged per session and required at least seven events in
one~\autocite{jadud2005thesis}.
Learnlog sessions are shorter than a BlueJ class period, so the
quotient for a period is the mean over all its pairs, pooled across
sessions; with no pairs there is no quotient, and the report shows a
dash rather than a zero that would read as \enquote{no repeated
errors}.

<<metrics functions>>=
def error_quotient(sessions, kind, scorer=score_pair):
    """Mean pair score over all pairs in `sessions`, or `None`."""
    pairs = [pair for session in sessions for pair in run_pairs(session)]
    if not pairs:
        return None
    return sum(
        scorer(first, second, kind) for first, second in pairs
    ) / len(pairs)
@

Let's verify the hand-computed values: a different-type error pair
scores $13/16$, a same-type pair $16/16$, a pair with a clean run
scores zero, and the mean of those three is $29/48$; Jadud's form
scores the same-type same-line pair $8/9$ without the edit term and
$9/9$ with it, and a pair of different types one line apart $5/9$;
an unchanged re-run forms no pair, and syntax errors do
not pair with runtime ones in the syntax series.

<<metrics test functions>>=
def test_error_quotient_two_parameter_values():
  session = [_syntax(0), _name(2), _name(4), _rec(6)]
  assert score_pair(session[0], session[1], "any") == 13 / 16
  assert score_pair(session[1], session[2], "any") == 1.0
  assert score_pair(session[2], session[3], "any") == 0.0
  assert error_quotient([session], "any") == pytest.approx(29 / 48)
  assert error_quotient([session], "syntax") == 0.0
  assert error_quotient([[_rec(0)]], "any") is None

def test_error_quotient_jadud_terms_and_zero_edit_filter():
  a, b = _name(0), _name(2)
  assert score_pair_jadud(a, b, "any") == 8 / 9
  assert score_pair_jadud(a, b, "any", same_edit_location=True) == 1.0
  assert score_pair_jadud(_syntax(0), _name(2), "any") == 5 / 9
  unchanged = [_name(0, tree="same"), _name(2, tree="same")]
  assert run_pairs(unchanged) == []
  assert error_quotient([unchanged], "any") is None
  other_file = [_name(0), _name(2, script="other.py")]
  assert run_pairs(other_file) == []
@


\section{Repeated error density}
\label{sec:metrics-red}

The error quotient scores \enquote{xx}, \enquote{xxx} and
\enquote{xx\ldots xx} alike; Becker's repeated error density separates
them and penalises one long string of the same error above two short
ones~\autocite{becker2016new}.
For each maximal string of $r_i$ consecutive repeated errors it adds
$r_i^2/(r_i+1)$, the square of the repeats over the length of the
string, and it was designed for sessions too short for the quotient
\autocite{becker2016new}---which learnlog's are.
It has no official normaliser and is unbounded above; the
reimplementations that divide it by something say so in a comment, and
we do not divide.
Its predictive power is weaker than the
quotient's~\autocite{svabensky2024comparison}: its value is diagnostic
granularity, and it is reported beside the quotient, not instead.

A \emph{repeated error} is two consecutive runs of the same script
ending in the same error type; the pairs are the ones the quotient
scores, so an unchanged re-run does not count as a repeat here either.

<<metrics functions>>=
def repeated_error_density(sessions, kind):
    """Becker's RED over all sessions, or `None` with no pairs."""
    total = 0.0
    seen_pair = False
    for session in sessions:
        chains = collections.defaultdict(list)
        for first, second in run_pairs(session):
            chains[first.script].append((first, second))
        for pairs in chains.values():
            repeats = 0
            for first, second in pairs:
                seen_pair = True
                before, after = (
                    classify_error(first), classify_error(second),
                )
                if (
                    is_error(before, kind) and is_error(after, kind)
                    and before.type == after.type
                ):
                    repeats += 1
                else:
                    total += _red_term(repeats)
                    repeats = 0
            total += _red_term(repeats)
    return total if seen_pair else None
<<metrics functions>>=
def _red_term(repeats):
    return repeats ** 2 / (repeats + 1) if repeats else 0.0
@

Let's verify Becker's worked values: one repeat scores $1/2$, two
separate repeats $1$, a string of two repeats $4/3$.

<<metrics test functions>>=
def test_repeated_error_density_worked_values():
  assert repeated_error_density(
    [[_name(0), _name(2)]], "any") == 0.5
  assert repeated_error_density(
    [[_name(0), _name(2), _rec(4), _name(6), _name(8)]], "any") == 1.0
  assert repeated_error_density(
    [[_name(0), _name(2), _name(4)]], "any") == pytest.approx(4 / 3)
  assert repeated_error_density([[_name(0), _rec(2)]], "any") == 0.0
  assert repeated_error_density([[_name(0)]], "any") is None
@


\section{Out of scope: Watwin and NPSM}

Two relatives of the quotient are not computed.
The Watwin score adds a penalty for how long the student took to
resolve an error, bounded by percentiles of \emph{the cohort's} resolve
times~\autocite{watson2013predicting,watson2015thesis}; a single
student's log has no cohort, and the published penalty values could
only be recovered from a reimplementation.
The normalized programming state model tracks the fraction of time
spent in each combination of syntactic and semantic
correctness~\autocite{carter2015npsm}; its semantic axis is exactly
what learnlog records (did the last execution raise?), but two of its
states need a debugger learnlog does not see, and it has not been
replicated.
Both are surveyed side by side with the quotient and the density by
Villamor~\autocite{villamor2020review}.


\section{Periods and buckets}
\label{sec:metrics-periods}

A report covers either one \emph{window} or a series of
\emph{buckets}.
The two are deliberately different shapes.
A window is rolling: \enquote{last week} means the last seven days,
because an ISO week is empty every Monday morning, and a default that
says nothing two days out of seven is the wrong default; \enquote{today}
is the local calendar day, since it is not a rolling notion.
Buckets are calendar units---days, ISO weeks, months---because a series
is read across its labels, and \enquote{2026-W35} is a name the student
can put beside a course schedule where \enquote{the seven days ending
Tuesday} is not.
An explicit [[--since]]/[[--until]] pair replaces the window; a report
whose header could describe two windows is worse than an error, so the
command layer refuses the combination.

<<metrics constants>>=
class Period(str, enum.Enum):
    """The rolling windows a single report can cover."""

    TODAY = "today"
    WEEK = "week"
    MONTH = "month"
    ALL = "all"


class Granularity(str, enum.Enum):
    """The units a series is sliced by."""

    DAY = "day"
    WEEK = "week"
    MONTH = "month"
    TAG = "tag"


PERIOD_DAYS = {Period.WEEK: 7, Period.MONTH: 30}
@

The two enumerations and the day counts are constants of the same
kind---vocabularies the command line and the reports share---so they
sit in the constants bucket, which also puts them ahead of the
functions that read [[PERIOD_DAYS]] when the module loads.

[[Granularity]] also lists [[tag]], though its arithmetic is nothing
like a calendar's (\cref{sec:metrics-tags}): [[--by]] asks
\enquote{one row per what}, and a milestone is an answer to that
question in the student's own vocabulary.
A separate option would give [[trends]] two ways to be asked the same
question; the price of one vocabulary is that the calendar helpers
below must refuse the member that is not theirs, and they do.

<<metrics functions>>=
def period_window(period, records, now=None):
    """Return the `(start, end)` datetimes of a rolling window."""
    now = now or datetime.datetime.now()
    if period is Period.TODAY:
        return midnight(now), now
    if period is Period.ALL:
        start = records[0].start if records else now
        return min(start, now), now
    return now - datetime.timedelta(days=PERIOD_DAYS[period]), now
<<metrics functions>>=
def midnight(moment):
    """The start of `moment`'s day."""
    return moment.replace(hour=0, minute=0, second=0, microsecond=0)
@

A boundary given as a date means the whole day: [[--since]] a date is
its midnight and [[--until]] a date runs through its end.
A boundary given as a datetime is taken as it is.

<<metrics functions>>=
def parse_boundary(text, *, inclusive_end=False):
    """Parse a `YYYY-MM-DD` or ISO datetime boundary."""
    try:
        moment = datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(text)
    except ValueError as error:
        raise ValueError(f"not a date or datetime: {text!r}") from error
    if inclusive_end and len(text) == len("YYYY-MM-DD"):
        moment += datetime.timedelta(days=1)
    return local_naive(moment)
@

A boundary the student types may also be one of their own tag
names---[[--since lab1]]---and the tag is tried \emph{first}: a tag
named like a date would otherwise be silently unreachable, while a
date is never shadowed by accident, only by a tag the student chose
to create in their own namespace.
Where the names come from and what moment a tag denotes is the
business of \cref{sec:metrics-tags}; here a name is only a key in
[[starts]].
The refusal lists the log's tags because the likeliest cause is a
typo in one of them.

<<metrics functions>>=
def resolve_boundary(text, starts, *, inclusive_end=False):
    """Resolve a boundary: a tag name, else a date or datetime.

    `starts` maps tag names to datetimes.  A `text` that is
    neither a known tag nor parseable raises `ValueError`
    naming the tags that do exist.
    """
    if text in starts:
        return starts[text]
    try:
        return parse_boundary(text, inclusive_end=inclusive_end)
    except ValueError as error:
        known = ", ".join(sorted(starts)) or "none"
        raise ValueError(
            f"not a date, a datetime, or a tag: {text!r} "
            f"(tags in this log: {known})"
        ) from error
@

A tag hits the map before [[inclusive_end]] can matter, so the
day-extension stays a fact about \emph{dates}: a date [[--until]] is
inclusive because a day means the whole day, while a tag [[--until]]
is that tag's exact start, exclusive---both say \enquote{up to the
next thing}, and for a tag the next thing is its own run.

<<metrics functions>>=
def explicit_window(since, until, records, now=None, starts=None):
    """Return the `(start, end)` of a `--since`/`--until` window.

    `starts` maps tag names to the start of their period; a
    boundary naming one resolves there before being read as a
    date.
    """
    now = now or datetime.datetime.now()
    starts = starts or {}
    start = resolve_boundary(since, starts) if since else (
        records[0].start if records else now
    )
    end = resolve_boundary(
        until, starts, inclusive_end=True,
    ) if until else now
    return start, end
<<metrics functions>>=
def filter_window(records, start, end):
    """The records whose start lies in `[start, end)`."""
    return [record for record in records if start <= record.start < end]
@

Sessions are cut over the whole window first and assigned to buckets
afterwards, each session going to the bucket of its \emph{first} run.
One rule instead of two keeps every metric additive across buckets;
its price is that a session begun at 23:40 on a Sunday contributes its
Monday runs to the Sunday bucket, and, at the other end, that a
session straddling the start of the window is cut there, so
[[--period week]] can show a shorter first session than
[[--period all]] does over the same runs.
Empty buckets between the first and last non-empty ones are emitted: a
week with no work is information, and the trend rule must see the
zero.
@

The week arithmetic has to agree with itself: [[bucket_start]] steps
back to Monday with [[weekday()]], and [[bucket_label]] names the
week with [[isocalendar()]], which also starts weeks on Monday---so
the first days of January can carry the previous year's label
([[2026-W53]]), and that is correct.

All three helpers spell out the [[MONTH]] branch and refuse anything
else.
Before [[Granularity.TAG]] existed the fall-through \emph{was} the
month branch; now a stray [[TAG]] falling through would be discovered
by a wrong answer instead of an error, and calendar arithmetic knows
nothing about tags.

<<metrics functions>>=
def bucket_start(moment, by):
    """The start of the calendar unit containing `moment`."""
    day = midnight(moment)
    if by is Granularity.DAY:
        return day
    if by is Granularity.WEEK:
        return day - datetime.timedelta(days=day.weekday())
    if by is Granularity.MONTH:
        return day.replace(day=1)
    raise ValueError(f"not a calendar unit: {by}")
<<metrics functions>>=
def next_bucket(start, by):
    """The start of the calendar unit after the one at `start`."""
    if by is Granularity.DAY:
        return start + datetime.timedelta(days=1)
    if by is Granularity.WEEK:
        return start + datetime.timedelta(days=7)
    if by is Granularity.MONTH:
        if start.month == 12:
            return start.replace(year=start.year + 1, month=1)
        return start.replace(month=start.month + 1)
    raise ValueError(f"not a calendar unit: {by}")
<<metrics functions>>=
def bucket_label(start, by):
    """The label of the calendar unit starting at `start`."""
    if by is Granularity.DAY:
        return start.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")
    if by is Granularity.WEEK:
        year, week, _ = start.isocalendar()
        return f"{year}-W{week:02d}"
    if by is Granularity.MONTH:
        return start.strftime("%Y-%m")
    raise ValueError(f"not a calendar unit: {by}")
<<metrics functions>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class Bucket:
    """The sessions of one calendar unit (or of the whole window)."""

    label: str
    start: datetime.datetime
    end: datetime.datetime
    sessions: list

    @property
    def runs(self):
        """Every run of the bucket, in order."""
        return [run for session in self.sessions for run in session]
<<metrics functions>>=
def bucket_sessions(sessions, by, window):
    """Slice `sessions` into `Bucket`s.

    With `by` `None`, one bucket labelled by the `window`
    holds everything.  Otherwise a session belongs to the
    calendar unit of its first run, and empty units between the
    first and last non-empty ones are included.
    """
    start, end = window
    if by is None:
        return [Bucket(window_label(start, end), start, end, sessions)]
    grouped = {}
    for session in sessions:
        key = bucket_start(session[0].start, by)
        grouped.setdefault(key, []).append(session)
    if not grouped:
        return []
    buckets = []
    current, last = min(grouped), max(grouped)
    while current <= last:
        following = next_bucket(current, by)
        buckets.append(Bucket(
            bucket_label(current, by), current, following,
            grouped.get(current, []),
        ))
        current = following
    return buckets
<<metrics functions>>=
def window_label(start, end):
    """A label for a rolling window."""
    return f"{start:%Y-%m-%d} to {end:%Y-%m-%d}"
@

Let's verify the calendar arithmetic where it is easiest to get wrong:
a month boundary at midnight, an ISO week that belongs to the previous
year, an empty bucket in the middle, and the inclusive [[--until]].

<<metrics test functions>>=
def test_bucket_sessions_month_boundary_and_gaps():
  late = [_rec(13 * 60 + 50)]          # 31 Aug 23:50
  spill = [_rec(13 * 60 + 50), _rec(13 * 60 + 65)]   # into 1 Sep
  october = [_rec(31 * 24 * 60)]       # 1 Oct 10:00
  window = (T0, T0 + datetime.timedelta(days=40))
  buckets = bucket_sessions([spill, october], Granularity.MONTH, window)
  assert [b.label for b in buckets] == ["2026-08", "2026-09", "2026-10"]
  assert len(buckets[0].runs) == 2
  assert buckets[1].sessions == []
  assert bucket_sessions([late], None, window)[0].label == \
    "2026-08-31 to 2026-10-10"

def test_bucket_labels_iso_week_and_day():
  new_year = datetime.datetime(2027, 1, 1, 9, 0)      # a Friday
  assert bucket_label(bucket_start(new_year, Granularity.WEEK),
                      Granularity.WEEK) == "2026-W53"
  assert bucket_start(new_year, Granularity.WEEK) == \
    datetime.datetime(2026, 12, 28)
  assert bucket_label(new_year, Granularity.DAY) == "2027-01-01"
  assert next_bucket(datetime.datetime(2026, 12, 1),
                     Granularity.MONTH) == datetime.datetime(2027, 1, 1)

def test_windows():
  now = T0
  assert period_window(Period.TODAY, [], now) == (midnight(T0), T0)
  assert period_window(Period.WEEK, [], now)[0] == \
    T0 - datetime.timedelta(days=7)
  assert period_window(Period.ALL, [_rec(-60)], now)[0] == \
    T0 - datetime.timedelta(hours=1)
  start, end = explicit_window("2026-08-01", "2026-08-31", [], now)
  assert start == datetime.datetime(2026, 8, 1)
  assert end == datetime.datetime(2026, 9, 1)
  assert filter_window([_rec(0), _rec(-10)], T0, T0 + IDLE) == [_rec(0)]
  assert resolve_boundary("2026-03-01", {"2026-03-01": T0}) is T0
  assert resolve_boundary("2026-03-01", {}) == \
    datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 1)
  with pytest.raises(ValueError) as caught:
    resolve_boundary("nosuchtag", {"lab1": T0})
  assert "lab1" in str(caught.value)
  with pytest.raises(ValueError):
    parse_boundary("last tuesday")
@


\section{Tag periods}
\label{sec:metrics-tags}

A student's tags name their milestones---[[learnlog tag lab2]] after
the first run of lab~2---and every reader treats a tag the way
[[git describe]] does: as a \emph{start marker}.
Tag [[T]]'s period covers [[T]]'s run and every run after it, until
the next tag.
One rule everywhere is the point: [[--since lab1 --until lab2]] is
exactly lab~1's period, [[--by tag]] is one row per period, and the
[[analyse]] report's sections cover the same runs
(\cref{sec:analyse-tags}).
The rule costs no new window arithmetic, because [[filter_window]]
is half-open: a boundary at the tagged run's start includes that run
under [[--since]] and excludes it under [[--until]], which is
precisely \enquote{up to the next milestone}.

A boundary needs a moment, and the honest moment is the start of the
earliest run recorded on the tagged commit---\emph{including}
learnlog's own runs, which [[run_records]] drops: a student who ran
[[learnlog list]], looked at their history and then tagged, tagged
\emph{then}, and the review run's start is that moment even though
no student program ran.
So the map below is built from the scans directly rather than from
[[run_records(include_learnlog=True)]], which would also compute
trees and churn no boundary needs, and whose churn attribution
depends on which runs are kept.
A commit with no readable run start---the repository's initial
commit, a foreign history---falls back to the commit's own date,
which is as accurate as anything else known about such a commit.

<<metrics functions>>=
def commit_run_starts(repo):
    """Map every commit to the start of its earliest run.

    Commits whose message yields no readable run start fall
    back to the commit's own date; commits without that are
    left out.
    """
    scan = scan_run_commits(repo)
    starts = {}
    for commit in get_commits(repo):
        message = merge_run_trailers(
            scan.messages.get(commit["hash"], ""),
            scan.trailers,
        )
        moments = [
            local_naive(_parse_iso(run["start_time"]))
            for run in parse_commit_runs(message)
        ]
        moments = [m for m in moments if m is not None]
        if not moments:
            fallback = local_naive(_parse_iso(commit["date"]))
            if fallback is None:
                continue
            starts[commit["hash"]] = fallback
        else:
            starts[commit["hash"]] = min(moments)
    return starts
@

Let's verify the two halves of that decision against a real
repository: a tag created right after a review run starts its period
at the \emph{review} run's moment, and a tag on the initial commit
still gets a boundary:

<<metrics test functions>>=
def test_tag_boundaries_uses_the_tagged_run_start(workdir):
  repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
  _record_run(repo, "main.py", T0)
  review_start = T0 + datetime.timedelta(minutes=5)
  _record_run(repo, "learnlog list", review_start)
  repo.git("tag", "lab2")
  boundary, = tag_boundaries(repo)
  assert boundary.names == ("lab2",)
  assert boundary.start == review_start

def test_tag_boundaries_falls_back_to_the_commit_date(workdir):
  repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
  first = repo.git(
    "rev-list", "--max-parents=0", "HEAD",
  ).stdout.strip()
  repo.git("tag", "epoch", first)
  boundary, = tag_boundaries(repo)
  assert boundary.names == ("epoch",)
  assert abs(boundary.start - datetime.datetime.now()) < \
    datetime.timedelta(days=1)
@

Several tags can share a commit---[[lab1]] and [[milestone]] on the
same run.
A deterministic order with one of them first would hand the
alphabetically last name the whole period and the other a zero-width
bucket, and a zero-width bucket is not the kind of emptiness worth
emitting: an empty week is information, an artefact of the
representation is not.
So tags on one commit share a single boundary whose label joins
their names the way the [[X-Tag]] column already does
(\cref{progsnap2}), and [[--since]]/[[--until]] still resolve each
name individually through [[tag_starts]].
A tag whose commit the recording branch does not reach has no place
on a timeline of runs; it is dropped here, and a boundary naming it
then fails through [[resolve_boundary]]'s listing of the tags that
do exist.

<<metrics functions>>=
TagBoundary = collections.namedtuple(
    "TagBoundary", ["names", "commit", "start"],
)

def tag_boundaries(repo):
    """Return the ordered tag boundaries, oldest first.

    Tags on one commit share one boundary, their names
    sorted; tags whose commit the recording branch does not
    reach are left out.
    """
    starts = commit_run_starts(repo)
    boundaries = [
        TagBoundary(tuple(sorted(names)), commit, starts[commit])
        for commit, names in get_tag_map(repo).items()
        if commit in starts
    ]
    boundaries.sort(key=lambda b: (b.start, b.names))
    return boundaries
<<metrics functions>>=
def tag_starts(boundaries):
    """Map each tag name to the start of its period."""
    return {
        name: boundary.start
        for boundary in boundaries
        for name in boundary.names
    }
<<metrics functions>>=
def boundary_label(boundary):
    """A boundary's bucket label: its names, semicolon-joined."""
    return ";".join(boundary.names)
@

The period windows are cut with a [[<=]]/[[<]] split on the window's
own start: a boundary at or before it joins the \emph{leading}
period, one strictly inside opens a new period, and one at or past
the end is out of the report entirely.
That split does the edge work by itself.
A tag exactly on the window start labels the lead and produces no
zero-width bucket; and when the window starts mid-history, the most
recent tag \emph{before} it owns the leading period---the work at
the window's start belongs to that milestone whether or not the
window shows the run that started it.
Only with no tag at or before the start is the lead labelled
[[UNTAGGED_LABEL]], imported from the exporter so that every command
spells the one idea one way.
Periods with no runs are emitted for the reason empty weeks are
(\cref{sec:metrics-periods}): a milestone with no work in the
window is information.

<<metrics functions>>=
def tag_bucket_windows(boundaries, window):
    """The `(label, start, end)` triples of tag periods.

    Covers `window` completely: the lead is labelled by the
    most recent boundary at or before the window start, or
    `UNTAGGED_LABEL` when there is none.
    """
    start, end = window
    before = [b for b in boundaries if b.start <= start]
    inside = [b for b in boundaries if start < b.start < end]
    labels = [
        boundary_label(before[-1]) if before else UNTAGGED_LABEL,
        *[boundary_label(b) for b in inside],
    ]
    cuts = [start, *[b.start for b in inside], end]
    return [
        (label, cuts[i], cuts[i + 1])
        for i, label in enumerate(labels)
    ]
@

Calendar buckets assign a whole session to the bucket of its first
run (\cref{sec:metrics-periods}); tag periods cut it at the
boundary instead.
The two kinds of boundary are not the same kind of fact.
Midnight is arbitrary with respect to the work, so a session
straddling it is one piece of work the calendar happens to cut; a
tag is a boundary the student declared, so a session straddling it
is two pieces of work with no break between them.
Cutting is also what makes [[--by tag]] agree with
[[--since lab1 --until lab2]], which already cuts at the window
edges via [[filter_window]], and what keeps the error quotient
honest: EQ scores consecutive run pairs \emph{within} a session, so
not cutting would score the step from lab~1's last run into lab~2's
first as a repeated error.
The price is that the [[sessions]] count stops being additive
across buckets---a session cut in two counts once on each
side---where the first-run rule kept it additive by construction.

<<metrics functions>>=
def tag_buckets(records, boundaries, window, idle):
    """Slice `records` into `Bucket`s cut at tag boundaries.

    Sessions are segmented within each period, so a session
    straddling a tag is cut there.
    """
    return [
        Bucket(label, start, end, segment_sessions(
            filter_window(records, start, end), idle,
        ))
        for label, start, end in tag_bucket_windows(
            boundaries, window,
        )
    ]
@

The header names the window; when a tag chose an edge, the name is
what the student typed and the dates are derivative, so the names
come first and the dates follow in parentheses.
The dates carry minutes because a tag boundary is a run start, not
a midnight.
[[window_label]] stays as it is---it doubles as the single-window
bucket label, and a row label is no place for a sentence.

<<metrics functions>>=
def window_header(window, since=None, until=None, starts=None):
    """The header's window text, tag names first when tags chose it."""
    start, end = window
    dates = f"{start:%Y-%m-%d %H:%M} to {end:%Y-%m-%d %H:%M}"
    starts = starts or {}
    if since in starts or until in starts:
        return f"{since or 'start'} .. {until or 'now'} ({dates})"
    return dates
@

