\section{Cyclomatic complexity, size, and the rest}
\label{sec:metrics-code}

McCabe defined complexity on the control-flow graph, $v(G) = e - n +
2p$, and then gave the rule everyone implements: \enquote{the
cyclomatic complexity of a structured program equals the number of
predicates plus one}, with a compound predicate counting once per
condition, since [[if c1 and c2]] is [[if c1: if c2]] in
disguise~\autocite{McCabe1976}.
Radon implements that rule for Python, and radon is what we use.
Its counting, read from its source rather than its documentation
(which disagrees on two points), is: one for the function; one per
[[if]], [[elif]] and conditional expression; one per [[for]] or
[[while]], plus one when the loop has an [[else]]; one per [[except]]
handler, plus one for a [[try]]'s [[else]]; one per boolean operand
beyond the first; one per [[for]] and per [[if]] in a comprehension;
one per [[match]] case, less one when a case is the wildcard; one per
[[assert]]; and nothing for [[with]], [[lambda]], [[else]] and
[[finally]].

What the number means is contested, and the report says so.
Shepperd objected that every decision weighs the same, that structure
is invisible to it, and that splitting a program into functions changes
the total arbitrarily~\autocite{Shepperd1988}; Jay et al.\ found it
nearly a linear function of lines of code~\autocite{JayEtAl2009};
Landman et al.\ re-tested that on seventeen million methods and found
the correlation only moderate at function
level~\autocite{Landman2016}.
Function level is where learnlog reports, so complexity is shown per
function beside the size, never alone and never as a mean: for novice
programs it is one in the great majority of
cases~\autocite{Keuning2017}, and the interesting event is the first
function where it is not.

Halstead's measures count operators and operands~\autocite{Halstead1977}.
Halstead never said which Python tokens those are; radon's answer is
five node types (binary, unary and boolean operators, augmented
assignment, comparison), so a first program that only calls
[[input]] and [[print]] has volume zero.
The maintainability index built on them,
$171 - 5.2\ln V - 0.23\,v(G) - 16.2\ln L + 50\sin\sqrt{2.46\,C}$
\autocite{ColemanEtAl1994}, inherits that: radon reports it rescaled
to $[0, 100]$ and returns 100 when the volume is zero.
Its authors already warned that comments inflate it \enquote{especially
in small modules}~\autocite{ColemanEtAl1994}, and we count multi-line
strings as comments (radon's [[multi=True]]), which is what a novice's
docstring is;
its constants have no derivation~\autocite{Heitlager2007}, and against
measured maintenance effort only size carried
signal~\autocite{Sjoberg2012,Morrison2023}.
Both are reported, because they are what a code report is expected
to contain; both are labelled as radon's definitions, and the index
comes with its caveat in the report header.

<<metrics imports>>=
import statistics
from radon.complexity import cc_visit
from radon.metrics import h_visit, mi_visit
from radon.raw import analyze as raw_analyze
<<metrics constants>>=
MI_CAVEAT = (
    "Maintainability index (radon): inflated by comments in small "
    "files and 100 for programs with no operators; read with care."
)
HALSTEAD_NOTE = (
    "Halstead volume uses radon's operator definitions (binary, "
    "unary and boolean operators, augmented assignment, comparison)."
)
@

Every radon entry point raises [[SyntaxError]] on a file that does
not parse, and many novice snapshots do not.
A file's metrics are therefore all-or-nothing with the parse error
recorded, and every summary below is over the files that parsed, with
the count of those that did not beside it.
A class block in radon carries its methods; the class's own complexity
is their mean, which is not a function and is not listed.

<<metrics functions>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class FileMetrics:
    """Radon's metrics for one file, or its parse error."""

    path: str
    parse_error: str | None
    loc: int | None = None
    sloc: int | None = None
    lloc: int | None = None
    comments: int | None = None
    blank: int | None = None
    functions: tuple = ()
    volume: float | None = None
    vocabulary: int | None = None
    maintainability: float | None = None
<<metrics functions>>=
def analyse_source(path, source):
    """Compute `FileMetrics` for one source text."""
    try:
        raw = raw_analyze(source)
        blocks = cc_visit(source)
        halstead = h_visit(source).total
        index = mi_visit(source, True)
    except SyntaxError as error:
        return FileMetrics(str(path), str(error))
    functions = []
    for block in blocks:
        for unit in getattr(block, "methods", None) or [block]:
            functions.append((unit.name, unit.lineno, unit.complexity))
    return FileMetrics(
        str(path), None,
        loc=raw.loc, sloc=raw.sloc, lloc=raw.lloc,
        comments=raw.comments + raw.multi, blank=raw.blank,
        functions=tuple(functions),
        volume=halstead.volume, vocabulary=halstead.vocabulary,
        maintainability=index,
    )
<<metrics functions>>=
def analyse_snapshot(root):
    """Summarise the Python files under `root` as a dict."""
    files = [
        analyse_source(path.relative_to(root), path.read_text(
            encoding="utf-8", errors="replace",
        ))
        for path in snapshot_files(root)
    ]
    parsed = [metrics for metrics in files if metrics.parse_error is None]
    complexities = [
        unit[2] for metrics in parsed for unit in metrics.functions
    ]
    indices = [metrics.maintainability for metrics in parsed]
    return {
        "files": len(files),
        "unparsable": len(files) - len(parsed),
        "sloc": sum(m.sloc for m in parsed) if parsed else None,
        "lloc": sum(m.lloc for m in parsed) if parsed else None,
        "comments": sum(m.comments for m in parsed) if parsed else None,
        "functions": len(complexities) if parsed else None,
        "max_cc": max(complexities, default=None),
        "complex_functions": sum(
            complexity > 1 for complexity in complexities
        ) if parsed else None,
        "volume": sum(m.volume for m in parsed) if parsed else None,
        "mi_min": min(indices) if indices else None,
        "mi_median": statistics.median(indices) if indices else None,
    }
@

