\section{Linting}
\label{sec:metrics-lint}

Keuning et al.\ ran a general-purpose analyser over two and a half
million student snapshots and found its most frequent findings
pedagogically irrelevant, its relevant ones rarely fixed, and
concluded that educators should \enquote{customize the tool by
selecting a small set of relevant issues}~\autocite{Keuning2017}.
Liu and Petersen did exactly that for Python by wrapping pylint with a
restricted, reworded rule set~\autocite{LiuPetersen2019}.
The set here is small on purpose: the pyflakes family (undefined
names, unused imports and variables, and their kin), long lines,
comparison with [[None]] or [[True]] by [[==]], a bare [[except]], a
[[lambda]] bound to a name, and ambiguous one-letter names.

Both linters ship with learnlog, and [[ruff]] is the one run by
default: it has become the Python community's standard linter (its
documentation at \url{https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/} lists PyTorch,
Jupyter and Apache Airflow among its users), it is two orders of
magnitude faster than pylint---which matters when a series lints one
snapshot per bucket---and it has stable rule codes and JSON output.
[[pylint]] stays as the alternative because it infers types and so
catches what ruff's syntactic checks cannot; a course that wants that
depth chooses it with [[--linter pylint]] for one report, or with
[[learnlog config linter pylint]] for the project.
The two rule sets are twins where twins exist---[[E731]] and
[[unnecessary-lambda-assignment]], [[A001]] and
[[redefined-builtin]]---so that switching tools changes the depth of
the analysis, not the questions asked; only ambiguous one-letter names
([[E741]]) have no pylint counterpart.
The default is spelled as a member of the enumeration, which is why
it sits beside the class rather than among the constants: the
constants are tangled first.
Each tool is looked for in the project's own [[.venv]] first, so that
a student's own installation wins, then beside the interpreter running
learnlog, where the bundled copies live, and last on the [[PATH]].
When the chosen tool is missing all the same, the report says how to
get it and shows a dash, because a metric that is unavailable is not
a failure of the student's program.
Expectations are kept modest in the prose: nudging students on quality
had them add more violations than they
removed~\autocite{Bobadilla2024}, and improvement concentrates where a
violation was made an explicit learning
outcome~\autocite{Ostlund2023}.

